**Read:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), chapter 5 in full; [Electricity and Magnetism — MIT 8.02T and TEAL notes](../sources/Electricity-and-Magnetism-MIT-8-02T.lingua#r_00CWY37VDJSBRF5D6A8BCD7SXT), Coulomb law and electric-field chapters.

For static point charges in vacuum, the field of q at displacement r is q r/(4π ε₀ |r|³). Force on a sufficiently small test charge is q_test E. The test-charge idea separates a source property from the particular probe, while reminding us that a real probe can disturb the distribution.

Place two equal positive charges symmetrically about the origin. The field at the midpoint cancels, although the potential there does not vanish with the usual reference at infinity. Move a positive probe slightly along the line and then perpendicular to it; the force directions differ. Cancellation at one point is not a stable three-dimensional trap in a charge-free electrostatic region.

Build vector sums before substituting magnitudes. Check signs, symmetry, the far-field limit and the near-source singularity. The point-charge model applies outside a source whose size is negligible for the requested accuracy. It does not explain atomic stability or authorize extrapolating classical point charges to arbitrarily small distances. Separate the tested range of a law from the idealization used to calculate with it.

